and our shutout tonight for the federal  court reporter at the Detroit News who  for the last two years has been breaking  some very big stories about one of this  country's most significant labor unions  the United Auto Workers the UAW Detroit  is a Union town and the annual Labor Day  Parade is a big event but the news  coverage this year was driven by the  revelation that the city's most powerful  union leader Gary Jones the President of  the United Auto Workers the UAW is the  subject of an FBI investigation of  alleged corruption a story broken by  Robert Snell the Detroit News there were  protesters union workers protesting you  know decrying the corrupt the reports of  corruption that have come out of federal  court Gary Jones marched for part of the  parade declined comment to reporters on  the scene and left before the parade was  finished Snell who covers the federal  courts for the Detroit News first  started working on the UAW corruption  story some two years ago there's a lot  of outrage in some circles the the the  workers fear that this is confirmation  of what they've suspected that some UAW  leaders might just be out for themselves  and not for the average blue-collar  worker so far nine people have been  convicted the latest this week in  federal court in Detroit the Union has  sought to portray the convictions as not  that significant I mean I think we're  fairly well sourced with you know people  close to the investigation and there  hasn't been a challenging of any of the  facts what what they have tried to focus  reporters on is that this is merely a  few bad apples who have been charged and  convicted and that this doesn't  represent a broader crisis for the UAW  but this past week Snell and his  colleagues briana novo and ian  Thibodeaux broke the story that the  union president himself  Jones was part of the investigation  with exclusive coverage of an FBI raid  on Jones's home during the search the  investigators had left the garage door  open at Gary Jones home and one of the  neighbors across the street who was  watching this dramatic federal search  unfold grabbed his binoculars and could  see agents in the garage with the open  garage door counting what this neighbor  described as watts of cash really sort  of a dramatic description of what was  unfolding on the scene today federal  agents descended on the homes of some  top officials with one of the nation's  largest unions the raid on the home of  the UAW president was picked up by news  organizations across the country who had  pretty much ignored the story until now  today FBI special agents searching  inside the Canton Michigan home of UAW  president Gary Jones it's been somewhat  of a lonely beats once we broke the news  that the FBI and IRS and labor  department were you know investigating  and searching multiple locations around  the country the the coverage mushroomed  you know you started to see the  financial wires and pretty much every  major newspaper Wall Street Journal  start to weigh in and cover the  developments from last week the  allegations are reminiscent of the  blatant corruption at the Teamsters  Union something I covered in my early  days as an NBC News correspondent except  now there are a lot fewer reporters  assigned to cover such stories it's  tough in the newspaper industry with you  know fewer resources but thankfully my  editors have stayed committed to the  story for over two years  so it's it's a matter of you know what  we're gonna devote resources to and this  is like you said a union town it's a big  story in Detroit and we've been staying  on top of it for two years plus and  there's more to come you think for sure  definitely  in a statement the UAW says the union  and as president Gary Jones have always  fully cooperated with the government  investigation and suggests what it calls  profit laden auto companies stand to  benefit from media leaks false  assumptions and political grandstanding  but from what we can see  none of that would apply to Rob Snell  and the Detroit News now that's our  program for tonight thanks so much for  joining us we'll see you back here again  and next week
 

